On Thu, 28 Dec 2006 18:46:59 +0100, <carlhnelson@gmail.com> wrote:
> What I'm wondering now is whether I should spend any
> significant amount of time trying to learn spells.  Should I use a wish
> for a helm of brilliance and risk encounters with mind flayers to raise
> my int?  This is my first barbarian (well 11th, but the first 10 don't
> count) and I could use some advice on what to expect for later in the
> game.  So far I can cast Force Bolt with a fail rate of 91%.  I'd like
> to be able to cast create monster and would love to be able to cast
> identify but perhaps those spells are not to be part of this game?  Any
> advice would be appreciated.

The question is "what do you want to do?".  If the answer is "just  
ascend", then no.  Barbarians certainly aren't meant to be spellcasters,  
and trying to make them spellcasting can be considered a volountary  
challenge just like trying to ascend with illiterate wizard.

Not minding the risk of toying with m.f. which you plan, you would  
probably have to wear HoB and robe.  This means that choices in your  
ascension kit are much broader (No helm of telepathy.  No amulet of LS or  
no twoweaponing (you have to get both MR and reflection).  And you  
probably would be able only to cast simple spells.  It's not worth it.   
You will deal much more damage with your artifact weapons than with the  
best spellcasting skills Barbarian can afford.

If you want a role that involves both melee and magic, them my advices  
are: priest, knight and monk.

